{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-64600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=12\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=9\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-64600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-9{y}^{′}-12y+15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+4y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-64600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8b6567be219f658696263315b6efd96727ad5be436f51e09a44a873800e8fbfc"} {"question" "q-64600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-64600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+4y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-64600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+4y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-64645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion with light damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+17y=40{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ second." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-64645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+17y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +17 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t).\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is an exponential force. The constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+2{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=-2,\\,Q=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)+2{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & ={e}^{-\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4(\\pi )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4(\\pi ))+2{e}^{-3\\pi } \\\\  & =2{e}^{-\\pi }({e}^{-2\\pi }-1)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-64645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8b6567be219f658696263315b6efd96727ad5be436f51e09a44a873800e8fbfc"} {"question" "q-64645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-64645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(\\pi )=(2{e}^{-3\\pi }-3)\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(\\pi )=2({e}^{-3\\pi }-4)\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(\\pi )=2{e}^{-\\pi }({e}^{-2\\pi }-1)\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(\\pi )={e}^{-\\pi }(3{e}^{-2\\pi }-2)\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(\\pi )={e}^{-\\pi }(4{e}^{-2\\pi }-1)\\,\\text{m}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-64645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(\\pi )=2{e}^{-\\pi }({e}^{-2\\pi }-1)\\,\\text{m}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94231" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion without damping?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94231" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7y={e}^{-4t}+t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94231" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8b6567be219f658696263315b6efd96727ad5be436f51e09a44a873800e8fbfc"} {"question" "q-94231" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94231" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{″}+36y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+7y={e}^{-4t}+t"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+\\pi {y}^{′}+2y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+4y={e}^{5t}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}-25y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94231" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+7y={e}^{-4t}+t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=16\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n8{y}^{″}=-16{y}^{′}-10y+4{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{4}y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8b6567be219f658696263315b6efd96727ad5be436f51e09a44a873800e8fbfc"} {"question" "q-94233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{″}+2y=4{t}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+4y=2{t}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{4}y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}+5y=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+\\frac{5}{2}y=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{4}y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(25t+ϕ)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$ It can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\omega =5.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8b6567be219f658696263315b6efd96727ad5be436f51e09a44a873800e8fbfc"} {"question" "q-94382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"} {"question" "q-94382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072466/state.json" "file_sha256" "23dda596987ad4e1cb7c30a2bb65a3bc62a0a3ae0959339f7f07d908c5cd86a2"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2645 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
